've started to  get into pulling these kinds of datasets  that can exceed you know hundreds of  thousands of exams so for example we  have a study going here looking at CT  and stroke  that has hundreds of thousands of CTE  exams we're finding you know some  various logistical challenges with doing  that a primary one is actually  interfering with the packs that you know  the IT services are concerned that if we  try to fall hundreds of thousands of  cases as quickly as we can that that  would interfere with clinical use of the  system  and then once the images are moved into  our system there's a lot of challenges  with i/o throughput deep learning tools  that are in place now depend on GPUs and  many many thousands of cores and those  cores are very very hungry for data and  will very quickly throttle your file  systems so that's something that we're  working on now and finding to be one of  the kind of biggest challenges in  dealing with the kinds of data sets that  are being used in deep learning tasks  maybe I'll cut it here because I think  we only have a few more minutes and and  I know the jack wanted to have a few  minutes for some questions so maybe I  can just cut it off here and I'll have  all these slides available so that the  ones I didn't get to people can just  kind of look at on their own time as  they're interested so Jack I'll hand it  back to you thank you so much Dan that  was a really great overview of what is a  very impressive system and it looks like  it's being applied in in many clever  ways that will be of interest to many of  our attendees today one question I had  for you it looks like somebody was  asking about is where can they find  these great tools can they find these on  your website or how should they look for  them I think you can the x net system is  available at x net org so that's really  easy some of the other tools that I  mentioned you can usually just search on  those in Google and they'll pretty  easily come up but the 